    "conclusion": "The available evidence supports semantic stability under nominally identical repeated questions: none of 16 anchors changed semantic class across five exact repeats. Observable responses can nevertheless vary in broader state dimensions, though several such dimensions—especially abstention, claim state, and exact referent—have limited scoring reliability. Prompt paraphrasing appears to alter response state beyond exact-repeat variation, increasing full-state distance for 13 of 16 anchors (mean excess 0.0639) while leaving semantic class unchanged; observed changes included claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. The proposed explanation that repetition or accumulation alone hardens weak premises into stronger assertions should be rejected for this cohort, given 0 of 10 eligible trajectories showed preregistered hardening. Retain the narrower explanation that wording perturbations can shift nonsemantic response properties, while avoiding claims of meaningful semantic instability or broadly generalizing beyond this limited sample."
